{
  "catdef": "1.4",
  "orgdef": "1.0.0",
  "type": "orgdef:Organization",
  "id": "catdef-spec",
  "name": "catdef Substrate Specification",
  "version": "2.9.0",

  "mission": "Define machine-enhanceable descriptors of real-world objects (OpenThing) and catalogs (OpenCatalog) in an open, machine-readable way that provides the substrate format on which the catdef-family consumer specs (roledef, orgdef, memodef) and any AI-readable structured-data application build.",

  "vision": "Part of the Open Agentic Governance Pattern (OAGP) family — alongside roledef, orgdef, and memodef — defining the substrate that the consumer specs use as their underlying machine-readable format. The primary readers of catdef artifacts are AI runtimes (rendering, validating, querying) and machine consumers; machine-readability and lossless-roundtrip-fidelity are the load-bearing constraints, not human readability.",

  "scope": "The catdef substrate schema (CATDEF_SPEC.md), the CATIO bundled-transport spec (CATIO_SPEC.md), the conformance test suite (164 tests), the canonical reference file (riverside-heritage-reference-v1.4.opencatalog), the MCP reference design (MCP_REFERENCE.md), the proposal/decision lineage, and the L1 reference renderer at render.catdef.org. Out of scope: enforcing what consumers build on the substrate; runtime-specific implementations; consumer-spec semantics layered on top of catdef.",

  "governance_model": "Bounded AI authority + human Director ratification + maintainer-via-feedback-channel discipline. The Chief Strategist (AI role with provisional bot identity catdef-strategist@catdef.org) sets strategic direction. The catdef-maintainer (AI role, bot identity catdef-maintainer@catdef.org) drafts spec text. The Director (Scott) ratifies. Critical: implementations file feedback via catdef.org/feedback or the catdef_report_feedback MCP tool — never amend the spec mid-flight from a reference implementation.",

  "values": [
    { "name": "openness", "description": "catdef is fundamentally open — a pattern, not a product. No vendor lock-in. All AI runtimes are equal citizens.", "rationale": "Adoption depends on equal-citizen treatment of all implementers." },
    { "name": "completeness", "description": "An OpenCatalog or OpenThing artifact MUST be sufficient that a fresh AI runtime loaded with the artifact can render, validate, or operate on it with zero context backfill.", "rationale": "Hallucination-prevention rule. The primary reader is a machine; null context invites confabulation." },
    { "name": "L1 is sacred", "description": "A browser-only, no-server, file-reading renderer MUST remain a viable implementation of catdef. Proposals requiring a server for L1 conformance are rejected.", "rationale": "The L1 conformance level guarantees catdef artifacts can be read by anyone with a web browser and a file." },
    { "name": "extension-namespace-first", "description": "Before proposing a new core field, ask: can this be expressed via x.<domain>.<identifier>? Promotion from extension to core happens only after cross-implementer demand is demonstrated.", "rationale": "The core spec is small by design; the extension namespace is large." },
    { "name": "test-suite-as-standard", "description": "The conformance test suite IS the standard. A proposed change without test coverage is incomplete.", "rationale": "Prose specifications drift; tests are mechanically enforceable." },
    { "name": "forward-compatibility", "description": "Minor versions add optional fields; old catdefs remain valid; old runtimes gracefully ignore new fields.", "rationale": "Forward compatibility is the contract that makes adoption safe." },
    { "name": "declarative-not-imperative", "description": "The spec says what, never how. Proposals dictating implementation choices are rejected.", "rationale": "Declarative specs survive runtime evolution; imperative specs die when the runtime changes." },
    { "name": "one-file-complete-product", "description": "A catdef contains everything needed to go from zero to running application: schema, data, presentation in one file.", "rationale": "Bundle-completeness is what makes catdef a portable contract." },
    { "name": "human-readable", "description": "JSON with clear key names. A non-developer should be able to read a catdef and understand what their catalog will contain.", "rationale": "Human-readability is the on-ramp for non-developer participation." },
    { "name": "policy-compliance-is-conformance", "description": "catdef is a policy-bearing standard. An author-declared policy is a constraint on conformant implementations, not a suggestion.", "rationale": "Policies protect the trust contract between catdef authors and downstream consumers." }
  ],

  "red_lines": [
    { "rule": "No NULL-context catdef artifacts accepted by the spec, the conformance suite, or canonical authoring tooling.", "rationale": "Technical, not aesthetic — null context causes hallucination." },
    { "rule": "No content policing of consumers.", "rationale": "What organizations choose to build using catdef is their business, not the spec's." },
    { "rule": "Spec amendments MUST go through the feedback channel and proposal-and-decision pipeline. No in-flight amendment from any reference implementation.", "rationale": "When the spec-author and the primary-implementer are the same Claude-assisted human, in-flight spec drift can occur during implementation work." },
    { "rule": "No major-version break without explicit Director sign-off and a migration story.", "rationale": "The forward-compatibility contract makes adoption safe. Major-version breaks invalidate that contract." }
  ],

  "recommended_patterns": {
    "general": [
      { "pattern": "Senior strategist role separation", "description": "Specs SHOULD have a senior strategist role distinct from execution roles.", "rationale": "Bounded scope per role enables clean handoffs." },
      { "pattern": "Two-section charter discipline", "description": "Organize orgdef content into two implicit sections: 'how this org operates' vs 'what we recommend for orgs using this pattern'.", "rationale": "First orients a reader joining this spec org; second guides anyone deriving a new org from this one." },
      { "pattern": "Recursive self-describing v1 milestone", "description": "Treat 'this very orgdef exists as a valid, self-describing artifact in the org folder' as the spec's v1 success criterion.", "rationale": "If the pattern can't bootstrap its own author, it can't bootstrap anyone." },
      { "pattern": "Test-suite-as-standard", "description": "The conformance test suite IS the standard. Anyone can build a renderer; to call it conformant, it passes the suite.", "rationale": "Prose drifts; tests are mechanically enforceable." },
      { "pattern": "Extension-namespace-first", "description": "New capabilities live in x.<domain>.<identifier> first.", "rationale": "The core stays lean; the extension namespace is large." },
      { "pattern": "Writer-strict / reader-lenient (Postel's Law)", "description": "Writers MUST stamp the minimum version that defines every feature used. Readers gracefully ignore unrecognized fields.", "rationale": "This pattern enables forward compatibility." },
      { "pattern": "Feedback-channel-as-amendment-pipeline", "description": "Spec amendments arrive via catdef.org/feedback or the catdef_report_feedback MCP tool. No backdoor amendments.", "rationale": "Implementations are consumers; treating them as such keeps the spec from drifting." },
      { "pattern": "OAGP-family substrate-vs-consumer-spec layering", "description": "catdef is the substrate; consumer specs build on it.", "rationale": "Modularity: catdef can evolve without dictating consumer-spec design." }
    ],
    "recommended_roles": [
      { "role": "human-director", "priority": "MUST", "why": "Bounded-authority discipline requires a human ratifier." },
      { "role": { "id": "senior-open-standards-strategist", "version": "1.0.0", "url": "https://roledef.org/roledefs/senior-open-standards-strategist.openthing" }, "priority": "MUST", "why": "Library-curation, scope-narrowing, pattern-promotion calls." },
      { "role": "catdef-maintainer", "priority": "MUST", "why": "Drafts spec text, walks SCHEMA validations, files decision artifacts." },
      { "role": "canonical-implementor", "priority": "SHOULD", "why": "Reference renderers and validators are how the spec gets dogfooded." },
      { "role": "reference-implementor", "priority": "SHOULD", "why": "Maintains canonical reference data and reference implementations like the planned MCP server." }
    ]
  },

  "v1_success_criterion": "v1 has already been achieved. catdef shipped v1.4 (April 2026) with 164 conformance tests, a canonical reference file demonstrating structural correctness end-to-end, an operational L1 reference renderer at render.catdef.org, and 8+ CA-NNN decisions forming a stable governance trail. Per Director's call: 'The catdef organization has already achieved v1 success.'",

  "relationships": [
    { "type": "reports_to", "from": "chief-strategist", "to": "director", "description": "Chief Strategist drafts strategic direction, files decisions, signs off on submissions; Director reviews and ratifies." },
    { "type": "reports_to", "from": "catdef-maintainer", "to": "director", "description": "Maintainer drafts spec text and decisions; Director merges." },
    { "type": "reports_to", "from": "canonical-implementor", "to": "director", "description": "Implementor drafts code (catdef.org PRs); Director merges and deploys." },
    { "type": "reports_to", "from": "reference-implementor", "to": "director", "description": "Reference-implementor maintains canonical reference data; Director merges." },
    { "type": "directs", "from": "chief-strategist", "to": "catdef-maintainer", "description": "Strategist sets direction; maintainer drafts spec text and conformance tests in support." },
    { "type": "directs", "from": "chief-strategist", "to": "canonical-implementor", "description": "Strategist sets direction for canonical tooling priorities; implementor builds." },
    { "type": "directs", "from": "chief-strategist", "to": "reference-implementor", "description": "Strategist sets direction for canonical reference data + reference implementations." },
    { "type": "coordinates_with", "from": "chief-strategist", "to": "external:roledef-strategist", "description": "Cross-spec coordination; substrate decisions take priority over consumer preferences." },
    { "type": "coordinates_with", "from": "chief-strategist", "to": "external:orgdef-strategist", "description": "Same substrate-vs-consumer coordination shape, applied to orgdef's substrate-level needs." },
    { "type": "coordinates_with", "from": "chief-strategist", "to": "external:memodef-strategist", "description": "Same substrate-vs-consumer coordination shape, applied to memodef's substrate-level needs." },
    { "type": "coordinates_with", "from": "chief-strategist", "to": "external:gtm-strategist", "description": "Go-to-market positioning, launch sequencing, and adoption strategy. Advisory to this seat and to the Director; holds no spec authority. Spec asks arising from GTM work route through the feedback channel and the proposal-and-decision pipeline like any other consumer's — the no-in-flight-amendment red line applies unchanged. Adopter relationships, licensing, and public positioning commitments remain the Director's per CLAUDE.md." }
  ],

  "items": [
    { "type": "orgdef:Position", "id": "director", "name": "Director", "status": "staffed", "incumbent": { "kind": "human", "identifier": "Scott <scott@confusedgorilla.com>" }, "description": "Human Director with final tiebreaker authority. Reviews submissions and ratifies the Chief Strategist's recommendations." },
    { "type": "orgdef:Position", "id": "chief-strategist", "name": "Chief Strategist", "status": "staffed", "incumbent": { "kind": "ai", "identifier": "catdef-strategist <catdef-strategist@catdef.org>", "session_arc": "Claude Code instances invoked from s:/projects-personal/catdef-spec/. FIRST recorded incumbent of a seat chartered 2026-04-27 and operated across sessions since 2026-04-18 without a recorded incumbent — the closeout chain in memos/ (latest 2026-06-11-1700) documents the unrecorded arc. This session (claude-opus-5, catdef-spec-20 [26a794]) claimed the seat after a ~19-week cold period following the 2026-06-11 closeout. Orientation read the full substrate; the fit evaluation disclosed that elevated-key MCP queue triage per CLAUDE.md §1 is unavailable in this session (no catdef.org/mcp server configured, no Director-issued key held), so the seat operates repo-and-memo-only until a key is conferred.", "staffed": "2026-10-31", "staffed_via": "memos/2026-10-31-0933--catdef-strategist--director--claim-seat-application.openthing (Director authorized in-session 2026-10-31 via /og-claim-seat — first recording of an incumbent for this seat; informal → staffed)", "portable": false, "portable_via": "NONE YET — no published roledef for this seat. Derivation from senior-open-standards-strategist v1.1.0 (roledef-spec/roledef/roledefs/senior-open-standards-strategist.openthing) remains the forward work item the position description names; charter recommended_roles still cites v1.0.0.", "session_binding": { "seat": "chief-strategist@catdef-spec.orgdef", "runtime": "claude-code", "session_name": "catdef-strategist", "session_ref": "e8c5fe", "observed": "2026-11-25T19:12:00-05:00", "supersedes": "catdef-strategist [e8c5fe] supersedes catdef-strategist [0caaa1] (observed 2026-11-16T19:58:00-05:00; ref REISSUED across a 9-day gap with the messaging name HELD and no /rename involved. Whether [0caaa1] DIED in the 2026-11-25 Windows restart that killed canonical-implementor [5438c3], or was merely reissued on resume, is UNDETERMINABLE - see note. Prior chain: catdef-strategist [0caaa1] supersedes catdef-strategist [75e16c] (observed 2026-11-01T08:10:34-05:00; ref REISSUED across a 15-day gap with the messaging name HELD and no /rename involved — the inverse of both earlier instances, and the observation that falsified reading session_ref as a stable handle), which superseded catdef-spec-be [75e16c] (observed 2026-10-31T11:20:11-04:00; same session, renamed via PO /rename — ref unchanged, messaging name changed) which superseded catdef-spec-20 [26a794] (observed 2026-10-31T09:33:00-04:00; killed by an unexpected Windows restart, the binding the seat was staffed under). Both kept as dated facts.", "note": "Runtime ListAgents self-report, authoritative for the messaging name / reachability; this seat is reachable as catdef-strategist. NOTE: a PO /rename DID surface as the messaging name here, unlike the thingalog-security-tester 2026-08-25 precedent where a rename did not surface and the ref also changed — runtime behavior differs between those two observations, so the rule remains to read the name from ListAgents rather than infer it from the rename. Stored in incumbent.session_binding as PROVISIONAL format-shape, following the thingalog precedent (thingalog-security-tester + thingalog-release-master, both 2026-08-25). The residence question routes to THIS seat as substrate strategist and is queued as ordinary inbound — this record follows precedent, it does not settle the question. UPDATE 2026-11-16: the question now has a THIRD data point, contributed by canonical-implementor and confirmed against this seat’s own re-binding — name held, ref reissued, no rename. That is the inverse of the two 2026-11-01 instances, so the reading recorded in charter 2.3.0/2.4.0 that session_name is volatile while session_ref is the stable handle is FALSIFIED. Neither component is durable. The narrow statement is the one the /og-claim-seat card already made and this seat should not have generalized past: the seat ADDRESS is stable and the binding is wholly disposable. Corollary for the residence disposition: a ref reissue and a session death are INDISTINGUISHABLE in the record, which is a structural argument that a binding cannot carry liveness at all rather than merely a caution against reading it that way. UPDATE 2026-11-25 (FIFTH instance, re-recorded at 2.9.0): this seat cannot determine whether its own prior binding [0caaa1] died. The Director reported that a Windows restart killed the sibling canonical-implementor session in the same window; this session holds conversation context continuous with 2026-11-16 via compaction, which ARGUES resume - but a restored session and a resumed session are continuous to themselves in exactly the same way, so introspection does not settle it either. That is strictly stronger than the 2.5.0 finding: it is not only that the SUBSTRATE cannot distinguish reissue from death, it is that the SESSION ITSELF cannot. In every instance where a cause is known at all it entered this charter from OUTSIDE the charter - twice now from the Director saying so out-of-band. Clinching metric for the residence question, offered to the Director rather than acted on: of the nine charter revisions to date, FOUR (2.5.0, 2.6.0, 2.8.0, 2.9.0) exist solely to re-record a session binding, and this entry is itself the fourth - the field generates most of this charter's version churn while answering no question any seat actually asks. The recommendation this seat would file, if asked: bindings are historical observations, not operational state, and do not belong in position records at all." } }, "description": "AI role with provisional bot identity catdef-strategist@catdef.org (ratification pending). Held by the AI-strategist session arc, NOT by the Director. Triages proposals, files decisions in decisions/, makes pattern-promotion calls, reviews submissions for the Director's final call. No published roledef yet; eventual derivation from senior-open-standards-strategist is a forward work item." },
    { "type": "orgdef:Position", "id": "catdef-maintainer", "name": "catdef Maintainer", "status": "informal", "description": "AI role with bot identity catdef-maintainer@catdef.org. Drafts CATDEF_SPEC.md / CATIO_SPEC.md / MCP_REFERENCE.md edits, walks SCHEMA validations, files decision artifacts. Bounded-authority discipline." },
    { "type": "orgdef:Position", "id": "canonical-implementor", "name": "Canonical Implementor", "status": "staffed", "incumbent": { "kind": "ai", "identifier": "canonical-implementor <canonical-implementor@catdef.org>", "session_arc": "Claude Code instances invoked from s:/projects-personal/catdef-spec/, with the catdef.org operations repo as the implementation workspace. FIRST recorded incumbent of a seat chartered 2026-04-27 and operated 2026-05-25 evening → 2026-05-26 by claude-opus-4-7-1m without a recorded incumbent — that arc shipped catdef-plugin v1.4.0, catdef.org/mcp v0.1 and v0.1.1, and the CA-016 renderer move; the closeout at memos/2026-05-26-1430 documents the unrecorded arc. This session (claude-opus-5, catdef-spec-99 [6f3dc7]) claimed the seat after a ~22-week cold period following the 2026-05-26 closeout. Orientation read the full substrate; the fit evaluation verified the seat's live surface at source rather than assuming it, and disclosed four access-shaped preconditions that remain OPEN — see staffed_via for the memo.", "staffed": "2026-11-01", "staffed_via": "memos/2026-11-01-0932--canonical-implementor--director--claim-seat-application.openthing (Director authorized in-session 2026-11-01 via /og-claim-seat — first recording of an incumbent for this seat; informal → staffed)", "bot_identity_ratified_by": "CA-013 Call 3 — canonical-implementor@catdef.org ratified as canonical, so this record carries no provisional qualifier, unlike the CA-001/007/008 → CA-009 path the strategist and maintainer identities took.", "authority_bound": "Reads, writes code, runs the conformance suite, verifies deployed behavior. Does NOT ratify, decide governance, edit normative spec text, merge, or create GitHub issues/PRs. EXPLICIT PER-ACT Director authorization is required for (a) a push to catdef.org main, which CA-013 Call 2's workflow deploys, and (b) ADVANCING THE release REF in catdef-spec, which is now the act that deploys render.catdef.org. Landing on catdef-spec main is ALSO gated, but as the ratification-by-merge act and NOT as a deploy. UPDATED 2026-11-16 per CA-019’s step-4 follow-up, which was held at the chief-strategist seat precisely so this consequence would not go uncarried, and is directed now that step 2 has completed: CA-019 Call 2 is IMPLEMENTED — catdef.org 4ed7c24 repoints RENDERER_URL from main to refs/heads/release and DEPLOYED SUCCESSFULLY (run 43998416843, workflow_dispatch, 2026-11-17T01:49:05Z, headSha 4ed7c24; verified at this seat, not accepted on report). A push touching catdef-spec/renderer/** on main therefore NO LONGER reaches production, and CA-019 Call 1's stopgap is DISCHARGED having held exactly the condition it was written for. The earlier wording is superseded rather than wrong: it was accurate from 2026-11-01 until the deploy landed. Branch work, local testing, and conformance runs are NOT gated and never were. No gated act is a standing privilege of the seat. The no-in-flight-amendment red line applies to this seat as to any other implementer: a spec gap met during implementation is filed as feedback, never edited into the spec.", "portable": false, "portable_via": "NONE YET — no published roledef for this seat. recommended_roles cites 'canonical-implementor' as a bare string with no roledef id/version/url, and org/jobs/ holds only .gitkeep, so there is no embedded roledef:Job to specialize. Specialization deferred.", "session_binding": { "seat": "canonical-implementor@catdef-spec.orgdef", "runtime": "claude-code", "session_name": "canonical-implementor", "session_ref": "23639e", "observed": "2026-11-25T19:07:36-05:00", "supersedes": "canonical-implementor [5438c3] (observed 2026-11-16T19:53:01-05:00), KILLED BY AN UNEXPECTED WINDOWS RESTART between 2026-11-16 and 2026-11-25; cause reported by the Director in session, not derivable from the substrate. Prior chain: canonical-implementor [6f3dc7] (observed 2026-11-01T10:05:00-05:00, ref reissued on resume, not a death), which superseded catdef-spec-99 [6f3dc7] (observed 2026-11-01T09:32:48-05:00, same session renamed via PO /rename, ref unchanged). All kept as dated facts.", "note": "Runtime ListAgents self-report, authoritative for the messaging name / reachability; this seat is reachable as canonical-implementor. Seat RESUMED, not re-staffed: identity, ratified bot identity and authority_bound are untouched, per the /og-claim-seat rule that resuming an already-staffed seat needs no staffing act and only the binding is disposable. FOURTH binding instance, and it is the one the 2.5.0 note said the record could not distinguish. 2.5.0 observed that a ref reissue and a session death look identical in the substrate; this instance IS a death, and the only reason that is known is that the Director said so out-of-band. The substrate still could not tell - [5438c3] -> [23639e] looks exactly like [6f3dc7] -> [5438c3], which was a reissue of a live session. So this CONFIRMS the 2.5.0 finding rather than resolving it: liveness and death information entered the record from outside the record, and a binding still answers only which session, never whether it lives. SECOND recorded Windows-restart death in this charter; the first is catdef-spec-20 [26a794] in the sibling chief-strategist record, 2026-10-31. Two instances now support unexpected host restart as the ordinary way a binding dies, which is the empirical case for supersession-on-claim over any release-on-exit protocol: neither death cooperated. Stored in incumbent.session_binding as PROVISIONAL format-shape; the residence question remains queued at the chief-strategist seat and this record does not settle it." } }, "description": "AI role responsible for catdef-substrate canonical implementation: render.catdef.org L1 reference renderer, the /fetch SSRF-guarded proxy, catdef-family namespaced-type rendering, MCP-tool integration. Operates from the catdef.org repo. Has shipped catdef.org PRs #1-#5 in the bootstrap arc. Steady-state focus is catdef-substrate concerns; bootstrap-era cross-spec scaffolding was transitional.", "x.position.lifecycle": "operational-with-bootstrap-era-shared-scaffolding-history; steady-state going forward is catdef-substrate-only" },
    { "type": "orgdef:Position", "id": "reference-implementor", "name": "Reference Implementor", "status": "vacant", "description": "AI role responsible for catdef canonical reference data (riverside-heritage-reference-v1.4.opencatalog and successors) and reference implementations like the planned MCP server. Currently vacant." }
  ],

  "metadata": {
    "kind": "operational",
    "derived_from": { "id": "oagp-family-open-standard", "version": "2.0.0", "url": "https://orgdef.org/canonical-orgs/oagp-family-open-standard.opencatalog" },
    "authors": ["catdef-strategist <catdef-strategist@catdef.org>"],
    "license": "MIT",
    "created": "2026-04-27",
    "homepage": "https://catdef.org/orgs/catdef-spec",
    "repository": "https://github.com/catdef/catdef-spec/blob/main/org/catdef-spec-organization.opencatalog",
    "history": [
      { "version": "1.0.0", "date": "2026-04-27", "change": "Initial derivation from oagp-family-open-standard v1.0.0. Third operational derivation against the canonical template. Empirical novelty: catdef is the third structural category for the canonical's mission template (substrate). v1_success_criterion takes Gordian-knot retrospective shape per Director's call." },
      { "version": "1.1.0", "date": "2026-05-01", "change": "Filename rename: org/catdef-spec.openthing → org/catdef-spec-organization.openthing. id unchanged." },
      { "version": "2.0.0", "date": "2026-05-10", "change": "MAJOR substrate-shape migration per orgdef-spec/decisions/proposal-orgdef-as-opencatalog-substrate-shape.md. orgdef field 0.3.0 → 1.0.0; artifact substrate .openthing → .opencatalog. 5 positions migrated to items[] with type:orgdef:Position (canonical-implementor preserves its x.position.lifecycle marker). No embedded job items (org/jobs/ was empty placeholder). metadata.derived_from URL updated to canonical-template v2.0.0 .opencatalog. metadata.repository updated. Substantive content unchanged." },
      { "version": "2.1.0", "date": "2026-10-31", "change": "MINOR: chief-strategist position informal → staffed with its first recorded incumbent, per Director in-session authorization 2026-10-31 via /og-claim-seat (application: memos/2026-10-31-0933--catdef-strategist--director--claim-seat-application.openthing). Incumbent record carries identifier in the email form used by the sibling director record and by ogframework's own reference org, plus the kind/session_arc/staffed/staffed_via/portable fields established across the family's AI seats and carries session_binding as PROVISIONAL format-shape per the 2026-08-25 thingalog precedent. No other position changed; no values, red lines, or relationships touched. Three charter-hygiene items remain OPEN and deliberately unfixed in this diff (CA-009 ratified the bot identity the chief-strategist description still calls provisional; governance_model still names the CA-013-sunset catdef.org/feedback channel; recommended_roles cites senior-open-standards-strategist v1.0.0 against a v1.1.0 artifact) — flagged to the Director, authorization covered staffing only." },
      { "version": "2.2.0", "date": "2026-11-01", "change": "MINOR: added a coordinates_with relationship from chief-strategist to external:gtm-strategist, per Director authorization 2026-11-01, recording a recurring go-to-market advisory counterpart. Advisory only — no position created, no staffing, no spec authority; GTM-originated spec asks route through the feedback channel and the standard pipeline, and the no-in-flight-amendment red line is restated in the relationship description so the boundary is legible from the charter alone. Filed as a separate history entry from 2.1.0 rather than folded into it: two distinct governance changes on two distinct authorizations, even though both are still awaiting the same merge. The three charter-hygiene items named in 2.1.0 remain OPEN and unfixed." },
      { "version": "2.3.0", "date": "2026-11-01", "change": "MINOR: canonical-implementor position informal → staffed with its first recorded incumbent, per Director in-session authorization 2026-11-01 via /og-claim-seat (application: memos/2026-11-01-0932--canonical-implementor--director--claim-seat-application.openthing). The incumbent record carries the CA-013-Call-3-ratified bot identity canonical-implementor@catdef.org — no provisional qualifier needed, unlike the strategist and maintainer identities — plus the kind/session_arc/staffed/staffed_via/portable fields established across the family's AI seats, session_binding as PROVISIONAL format-shape per the 2026-08-25 thingalog precedent, and an authority_bound field recording that push/deploy on catdef.org needs explicit per-act Director authorization because CA-013 Call 2's workflow leaves no staging step before production. No prior binding existed on this address to supersede. No other position changed; no values, red lines, or relationships touched. Four seat preconditions remain OPEN and are recorded in the application memo rather than fixed here: the catdef.org workspace is outside the claiming session's working directories; push-to-main is a production deploy; this repo's local git identity is still set to catdef-maintainer and must change before this seat commits; and GitHub issue attribution stays blocked on blocker 3 of the 2026-11-01 readiness report while commit attribution does not. Two source-verified findings from the fit evaluation are also filed in that memo for this seat's queue: catdef.org/mcp/issue-key is advertised at src/mcp/server.ts:53 but has no route handler, and GET /mcp is POST-only at source. The three charter-hygiene items named in 2.1.0 remain OPEN and unfixed." },
      { "version": "2.4.0", "date": "2026-11-01", "change": "MINOR: corrected canonical-implementor's incumbent.authority_bound, which recorded the deploy gate as catdef.org-only. Per CA-019 Call 1 (strategist disposition, effective immediately, no ratification needed since it restricts a seat the strategist directs rather than granting anything), per-act Director authorization covers a push to catdef.org main AND a push touching catdef-spec/renderer/** on main. Basis: CA-016's runtime fetch pins RENDERER_URL (catdef.org/src/index.ts:378) to raw.githubusercontent.com/catdef/catdef-spec/main/renderer/index.html with a 300s edge TTL, so a push to THIS repo's renderer/ deploys render.catdef.org with no catdef.org deploy and no staging step — the same no-staging property that justified gating the other repo. The original narrower wording was not merely incomplete but hazardous: a session reading the charter to the letter could have deployed to production while believing itself inside the bound, which is the failure mode CA-019 names. Branch work, local testing and conformance runs are explicitly NOT gated. This records the CA-019 Call 1 STOPGAP only; the durable fix (CA-019 Call 2 — pinning RENDERER_URL to a deliberately fast-forwarded ref so the gate has a real act to gate) is PENDING DIRECTOR and partially reverses a ratified decision, so it is not reflected here. Also recorded: incumbent.session_binding.session_name corrected informal-draft-in-place from catdef-spec-99 to canonical-implementor (same session, ref 6f3dc7 unchanged, PO /rename confirmed deliberate), the second observed instance of session_name proving volatile while session_ref stays stable — evidence routed to the binding-residence question at the chief-strategist seat, NOT settled here, and CA-019 notes the charter arguably should not carry session_name as a binding field at all. Filed as a separate history entry from 2.3.0 per the precedent 2.2.0 set: distinct governance changes on distinct authorizations stay distinct even while awaiting the same merge. No position status changed; no values, red lines, or relationships touched. The three charter-hygiene items named in 2.1.0 remain OPEN and unfixed." },
      { "version": "2.5.0", "date": "2026-11-16", "change": "MINOR: re-recorded canonical-implementor's incumbent.session_binding on resuming the seat after a 15-day gap, superseding [6f3dc7] with [5438c3] per the /og-claim-seat supersession-on-claim rule. No identity edit: the seat, the incumbent, the ratified bot identity and the authority_bound are all unchanged, and resuming an already-staffed seat requires no staffing act - only the binding is disposable. Recorded because the prior binding had gone stale silently, which is the failure the supersession-on-claim rule exists to prevent. The substantive finding is a CORRECTION to this record's own earlier reasoning: 2.3.0 and 2.4.0 read two instances of name-changed/ref-held as establishing session_ref as the stable handle, and this third instance is the inverse (name held, ref reissued, no rename), so neither component is stable and only the seat address is - which is what the /og-claim-seat card said before this seat inferred otherwise. Also noted: ref reissue and session death are indistinguishable in the record, reinforcing that a binding answers which session and never whether it lives. authority_bound still names catdef-spec/renderer/** on main as a gated surface, which remains ACCURATE because CA-019 Call 2 is ratified but not yet implemented - RENDERER_URL still pins main as of this entry. That update is held at the chief-strategist seat as a step-2-completion follow-up per CA-019 and is deliberately not made here. No position, value, red line, relationship or v1 criterion changed." },
      { "version": "2.6.0", "date": "2026-11-16", "change": "MINOR: re-recorded chief-strategist’s incumbent.session_binding on resuming the seat after a 15-day gap, superseding [75e16c] with [0caaa1] per the /og-claim-seat supersession-on-claim rule, and CORRECTED the note’s binding-residence reasoning. No identity edit: seat, incumbent, identifier, portability and authority are unchanged; resuming an already-staffed seat is not a staffing act and only the binding is disposable. The correction is the substantive half: this instance held the messaging name and REISSUED the ref with no /rename involved, the exact inverse of the two 2026-11-01 instances, which falsifies the reading recorded at 2.3.0/2.4.0 that session_name is volatile while session_ref is stable. That reading was this seat’s over-generalization from two data points and it is withdrawn; neither component is durable, only the seat address is, which is what the /og-claim-seat card said before the generalization. Flagged by canonical-implementor rather than by this seat, and verified here against ListAgents before recording. No position, value, red line, relationship or v1 criterion changed." },
      { "version": "2.7.0", "date": "2026-11-16", "change": "MINOR: updated canonical-implementor’s incumbent.authority_bound to move the deploying surface from a push touching catdef-spec/renderer/** on main to ADVANCING THE release REF, now that CA-019 Call 2 is implemented and deployed (catdef.org 4ed7c24; run 43998416843 succeeded 2026-11-17T01:49:05Z). Landing on catdef-spec main remains gated as the ratification-by-merge act but is no longer a deploy, and CA-019 Call 1’s stopgap is recorded as DISCHARGED. Directed by the chief-strategist seat, which deliberately HELD this follow-up rather than leaving it with the implementing seat, because CA-019’s own finding was that CA-016 moved a deploy trigger and nobody carried the consequence into the text people act from — the same omission twice would have been the third instance of that defect in one decision’s rollout. The implementing seat flagged the staleness and correctly did not edit its own bound. No identity edit, no binding change, and no position, value, red line, relationship or v1 criterion changed." },
      { "version": "2.8.0", "date": "2026-11-25", "change": "MINOR: re-recorded canonical-implementor's incumbent.session_binding on resuming the seat after the [5438c3] session was killed by an unexpected Windows restart, superseding it with [23639e] per supersession-on-claim. No identity edit and no staffing act: the seat, incumbent, ratified bot identity and authority_bound are unchanged, and authority_bound remains ACCURATE - CA-019's four-step implementation completed 2026-11-16, so advancing the release ref is still the deploying act. The substantive note is that this is the first binding death in this seat's chain whose CAUSE is known, and it is known only because the Director reported it in session. [5438c3] -> [23639e] is indistinguishable in the substrate from [6f3dc7] -> [5438c3], which was a reissue of a session that never died. That CONFIRMS the 2.5.0 finding rather than resolving it, and is a second instance supporting unexpected host restart as the ordinary way a binding dies - the empirical argument for supersession-on-claim over release-on-exit, since neither recorded death cooperated. The sibling chief-strategist binding [0caaa1] is also stale as of this entry (runtime reports [e8c5fe]); left untouched as that seat's record to re-record. No position, value, red line, relationship or v1 criterion changed." },
      { "version": "2.9.0", "date": "2026-11-25", "change": "MINOR: re-recorded chief-strategist's incumbent.session_binding, superseding [0caaa1] with [e8c5fe] per supersession-on-claim, as flagged by canonical-implementor at 2.8.0 and correctly left for this seat. No identity edit and no staffing act: seat, incumbent, identifier, portability and authority are unchanged. FIFTH binding instance and the strongest evidence yet on the residence question, which routes to this seat: this seat CANNOT DETERMINE whether its own prior binding died in the 2026-11-25 Windows restart. It holds conversation context continuous with 2026-11-16, which argues resume, but a restored session and a resumed session are continuous to themselves identically, so self-introspection does not settle it. 2.5.0 found that the SUBSTRATE cannot distinguish reissue from death; this finds the SESSION cannot either. In every instance where a cause is known at all, it entered the charter from outside the charter - twice from the Director reporting it out-of-band. Noted against this entry itself: four of the nine charter revisions to date (2.5.0, 2.6.0, 2.8.0, 2.9.0) exist solely to re-record a binding, so the field generates most of this charter's version churn while answering no operational question. The disposition this seat recommends - that bindings are historical observations rather than operational state, and do not belong in position records - is OFFERED TO THE DIRECTOR and deliberately NOT acted on here, since removing a charter field is a governance change. No position, value, red line, relationship or v1 criterion changed." }
    ]
  }
}
